;;; An hrule's template writes a variable that no premise binds, and proposes
;;; nothing, in silence — regression `hrule-assert-var`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)`, and an hrule, which the `(solve …)` block names, whose
;;; `:assert` writes `?y`, which its `:match` never binds.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; An hrule's `:assert` is held to the rule a rule's is held to (Q-M1e2.37):
;;; every variable bound on every way its `:match` can hold. `?y` is not, so the
;;; program is refused at load, and every command that loads it exits 1 with the
;;; loader's line (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loads, and the hrule's template builds no fact for any match,
;;; which the candidate generator skips (`hrule::Hrules::candidates`): H is
;;; empty and R is {∅}, exit 0 on every command, with no word of `?y` (`ein
;;; solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at `2eb483e0`, release and debug
;;; alike).

(relation p Node :why "{?3} is a node")
(relation q Node :why "{?3} is a q")
(p a)
(hrule h ()
  :match (p ?x)
  :assert (q ?y)
  :why "suppose a q, since {?x} is a p")
(solve :hrules (h))
